---
/**
 * Overland travel, shown rather than described.
 *
 * The formation guide explains how to drive each of these; this page exists to
 * answer the question a guide cannot, which is "what does a day of wilderness
 * travel actually look like on the sheet". One plate per surface, each from a
 * live game.
 *
 * Shots are staged out of `docs/releases/` by tools/sync.mjs, so a plate here
 * is the same file the release captured — there is no second copy to drift.
 */
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

type Shot = { default: ImageMetadata };
const shots = import.meta.glob<Shot>("../assets/shots/**/*.png", { eager: true });

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const guide = `${base}guides/formation/`;

const plates = [
  {
    shot: "v5.3.0/formation-weather.png",
    title: "The sky, and what it costs",
    body:
      "Weather is settled once a day for the ground you are standing on, derived from the date and " +
      "the climate rather than stored on whoever happened to roll it — so two parties in the same " +
      "country get the same day. The conditions it produces are named, and every one of them shows " +
      "up in the march beneath: forest, a road, snowfall, wind and snow underfoot, each as its own " +
      "factor. Mud and snow accumulate and dry out on their own.",
    alt:
      "The travel panel's weather controls — climate, season, temperature, precipitation, wind and " +
      "ground state — above the conditions they produced and a day's march of three miles with " +
      "every factor itemized.",
  },
  {
    shot: "v5.3.0/formation-camp.png",
    title: "The camp",
    body:
      "How many days the packs hold for everyone in the order, who is going short, and what tonight's " +
      "chosen hours are worth. Food and water are pooled across the whole party and dealt by a policy " +
      "you choose. The hours in the weather are yours to declare — a marching day is not automatically " +
      "a day spent unprotected, so the module does not assume one.",
    alt:
      "The camp section: days of food and water for three people, a field for hours spent in the " +
      "weather with at-a-fire and got-wet toggles, and the night's forage, water, firewood and " +
      "hunting throws each priced.",
  },
  {
    shot: "v5.3.0/formation-flight.png",
    title: "Flying, and what a flier skips",
    body:
      "How the order moves decides which factors it meets at all. Pick flying and two more fields " +
      "appear — how much of the day was spent aloft, and what the flier is carrying. A flier still " +
      "meets the ground below it, ignores roads entirely, and feels wind by its own rule rather than " +
      "the one that slows a walker. The readout names which factor replaced which.",
    alt:
      "The journey pickers with Moving by set to Flying, showing hours aloft, the length of the day, " +
      "and the flier's load alongside the ground, road and territory.",
  },
  {
    shot: "v5.3.0/formation-city.png",
    title: "In the city",
    body:
      "A settlement is crossed the way a wilderness is, at a different scale: a pace, the avenues or " +
      "the alleys, how well the party knows the way, and whether it is dark. Take a turn and the party " +
      "covers its blocks, tests its way, and the street gets its own chance at them. A party that goes " +
      "looking for trouble finds it more easily — the throw eases, it does not come round sooner.",
    alt:
      "The settlement board: pace, streets, route knowledge, after dark, drawing attention set to " +
      "looking for trouble, and how the party is carried — with the blocks covered, the navigation " +
      "throw and the street's own cadence beneath.",
  },
  {
    shot: "v5.3.0/formation-journey.png",
    title: "A day, end to end",
    body:
      "The whole Travel tab as a Judge sees it: the ground and the sky at the top, the day board and " +
      "its four ancillary hours, the march with every factor named, the camp below, and the log of " +
      "days already walked. End day throws for navigation, feeds the order, walks every body's hunger, " +
      "thirst and exposure one step, and writes the day down.",
    alt: "The complete travel tab, from the ground and weather pickers through the day board and march to the camp.",
  },
].map((p) => {
  const image = shots[`../assets/shots/${p.shot}`]?.default;
  if (!image) throw new Error(`exploring: no staged screenshot for ${p.shot}`);
  return { ...p, image };
});
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "Overland travel",
    description:
      "What a day of wilderness travel looks like in ACKS II — Extras: weather off the calendar, " +
      "pooled provisions, hunger, thirst and the cold, foraging and searching, flight, city travel, " +
      "and getting quietly lost.",
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <p>
    A journey in ACKS II is a sequence of small, costly decisions — how fast to
    march, what to spend the day's spare hours on, whether the party can afford
    to stop. This is what the module makes of that: a day board you fill in, a
    march derived with every factor named, and a camp that keeps track of what
    the country and the weather are doing to the people in it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every figure behind these surfaces — each threshold, target, penalty, yield
    and rate — arrives through <a href="/foundryvtt-acks-extras/guides/importer/">the
    importer</a> from your own books. Until you have imported them the panel
    still works and says plainly what it cannot price. It never guesses a
    distance, a target or a toll.
  </p>

  {
    plates.map((p) => (
      <section class="plate">
        <h2>{p.title}</h2>
        <p>{p.body}</p>
        <Image src={p.image} alt={p.alt} loading="lazy" />
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="plate">
    <h2>Getting lost</h2>
    <p>
      The one surface with no screenshot, because the whole point is that the
      players cannot see it. A failed navigation throw is whispered to the Judge,
      not announced. The party walks on believing it is somewhere it is not, and
      the map fills in with the ground they <em>think</em> they crossed — while
      the Judge alone watches a hidden marker move across the country they are
      really in.
    </p>
    <p>
      When they realise, that imagined ground closes back over and they are told,
      in as many words, that the last few days were not where they thought. They
      do not learn where they went. Finding their way back to a known landmark is
      what puts the country they really crossed onto the map, in its right place.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p>
    <a href={guide}>The formation guide</a> explains how to drive all of this,
    step by step.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
  .plate {
    margin-block: 2.5rem;
  }
  .plate h2 {
    margin-block-end: 0.4rem;
  }
  .plate img {
    border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-gray-5);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    margin-block-start: 1rem;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
